## Gap and evidence

The `ProducerConnection` Set-type correction had direct getter/setter alias
coverage for `ConsumerConnection`, but not for `ProducerConnection` itself.
Pinned Java's `setConnectionSet(HashSet)` retains the assigned object;
`getConnectionSet()` returns it, and insertion through that getter is visible
through the assigned reference.

The Java oracle now invokes this behavior on an isolated `ProducerConnection`
and records both reference identity and mutation visibility. The isolated DTO
keeps the separate producer RPC response fixture at its original one-entry
shape. Rust's Protocol regression performs the same setter/getter operation,
checks `SharedValue::ptr_eq`, and observes the inserted connection through the
assigned shared set.

Updated `fixtures/producer/core.json` SHA-256:
`412fb8f5d9805edb2f994876d81ca980bfccc2963a1aff2bea19b98319a1eb5a`.

An initial combined fixture probe mutated the DTO later used for the RPC
response and correctly caused the one-entry Rust response assertion to fail.
The oracle probe was moved to its own DTO; the regenerated fixture then retained
the one-entry RPC body and the Protocol suite passed.

